/*
:name: wire_while_loop_write
:type: error
:error: loop body may not drive wire 'back'
:help: drive the wire outside the loop
*/

// User ruling 2026-09-27 (ruling 5), `while` spelling: a `for`/`while`/`loop`
// body may READ a `wire` (the loop is then unrolled) but may not WRITE (drive)
// one -- a write there adds one driver per iteration (docs 04-variables.md and
// 05b-statements.md "Loops and `wire`"). errors/wire_loop_write.prp pins the
// `for` spelling; this pins `while` (prp2lnast prefixes the message with
// `loop`, and `loop { }` desugars to the same node).
//
// The body first READS the wire `src`. That read is legal after the ruling, so
// the diagnostic must name the DRIVE of `back`. Before the ruling, prp2lnast
// rejected the read:
//   loop body may not read or write wire 'src'   (on the `const s` line)
// The wording now that reads are legal:
//   message: loop body may not drive wire 'back'
//   hint:    drive the wire outside the loop; a loop body may only read a wire
mod wire_while_loop_write(a:U8) -> (z:U8@[0]) {
  wire src:U8 = nil
  wire back:U8 = nil
  mut i = 0
  while i < 1 {
    const s = src ^ i       // a READ of a wire: legal
    back = s  // locate_error_here
    i += 1
  }
  src = a
  z = back
}
